# 64-bit SimHash for near-duplicate documents

`stdlib/simhash.ltl` turns text into a compact fingerprint whose Hamming
distance is useful for finding documents that are almost the same.

## Tokenization

`simhash_fingerprint` lowercases ASCII letters and splits on whitespace and
punctuation. Each token is hashed with the standard-library xxHash64 string
helper. Repeated tokens contribute repeatedly, so the fingerprint is weighted
by token frequency.

## The 64-bit fingerprint

For every token, each of the 64 hash bits adds `+1` or `-1` to its accumulator.
An accumulator at least zero becomes a one bit in the final fingerprint. This
is Charikar's SimHash construction: similar token distributions tend to keep
similar bit votes.

## Comparing documents

`simhash_hamming(a, b)` counts differing bits. Identical documents have
distance zero. `simhash_near_duplicate(text_a, text_b, threshold_bits)` is a
convenience predicate; a threshold of 3--8 is a useful starting point for
short, lightly edited text, but should be calibrated on the document corpus.

```ltl
let a = simhash_fingerprint("A short article about trees")
let b = simhash_fingerprint("A short article about tree")
print(simhash_hamming(a, b))
print(simhash_near_duplicate("A short article about trees",
                             "A short article about tree", 8))
```

See `examples/simhash_tutorial_example.ltl` for batch clustering.
